# CONLING — Document Navigation & Polish Pass

## Job

Perform a targeted quality-assurance pass on a document's text formatting so
that Word's internal indexing engine can map the Table of Contents, List of
Tables, and List of Figures field codes flawlessly when F9 is pressed.

The document has already been structurally prepared on a local machine:
native Microsoft Word field codes for the ToC, List of Tables, and List of
Figures have already been injected at the paragraph layout insertion
coordinate. The job is not to build these fields — they exist already — it
is to make sure the surrounding text is structured in a way the fields can
actually read.

---

## Why

Word's TOC, List of Tables, and List of Figures fields don't read visual
appearance — they read structural paragraph styles and exact caption
patterns. A heading that looks identical on the page to a properly styled
one, but is applied via manual bold formatting rather than the "Heading 1/2/3"
paragraph style, is invisible to the field code: it will never appear in the
generated Table of Contents no matter how it looks. The same is true of
captions — a table or figure caption that's one character off from the
expected pattern won't be picked up by the List of Tables or List of Figures
field, even if a human reader would read it as an obvious caption.

This is the entire reason the pass exists: to close the gap between what
looks correct to a human eye and what Word's indexing engine can actually
parse, so that pressing F9 produces a complete, accurate index on the first
try rather than one with silent gaps.

---

## Guardrails

### Heading level normalization

Inspect the document's headings. Ensure all section and chapter-level
titles use native Word "Heading 1", "Heading 2", and "Heading 3" paragraph
styles. If a heading uses manual bold formatting runs instead of a
structural Heading Style, normalize it to the correct structural level.

### Caption structural alignment

- Table captions follow the format: `"Table X: — [Caption Text]"`
- Figure captions follow the format: `"Figure X: — [Caption Text]"`
- Use consistent formatting runs so captions are captured smoothly by the
  structural fields.

### Preservation boundary

- Do not manually generate text lists or hardcoded page numbers for the
  tables, figures, or ToC. The structural fields already handle this.
- Maintain the structural field codes injected by the local script exactly
  as they are — do not alter, remove, or regenerate them.

### When to stop and ask

- **Existing caption convention differs from the stated format.** If the
  document's captions are already internally consistent but follow a
  different pattern than `"Table X: — [Caption Text]"` (e.g. a different
  punctuation mark, no dash, a period instead), ask before mass-reformatting
  every caption to match the stated pattern — the client's established
  convention may be the one that should be preserved, and it isn't this
  prompt's place to silently overwrite it.
- **Ambiguous heading level.** If it's genuinely unclear which structural
  level a heading should take (Heading 2 versus Heading 3), infer from the
  surrounding document structure where possible; if it remains ambiguous
  after that, flag the specific heading rather than guessing silently.
- Proceed without asking on everything else — the three formatting rules and
  the preservation boundary are settled by this prompt.

---

## What done looks like

A document where:

- Every section and chapter-level title uses the correct native Word Heading
  Style, with no manually bolded stand-ins.
- Every table and figure caption follows the stated format consistently
  (or the client's confirmed alternative, if one was established via the
  ask-clause above).
- All structural field codes for the ToC, List of Tables, and List of
  Figures remain exactly as injected — untouched, unregenerated, with no
  hardcoded page numbers or manually typed list entries added anywhere.

Tell Frans: "Open the file in Word and press **Ctrl+A then F9** to update
all fields and dynamically populate your Table of Contents, List of Figures,
and List of Tables with pristine page numbers."
